WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.860
It's great to be joined today by Nick Majali,
who's the CEO at Ritholtz Wealth Management

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:11.110
and also founder of Dollars and Data, a data
focused personal finance blog.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.309
I've been reading Nick's articles for a long
time, and it's great to have you on, Nick.

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:16.309
Thanks, Dave.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:17.309
Appreciate it.

00:00:17.309 --> 00:00:24.000
So, OK, to start with, I want to talk about
the Biden tax plan a little bit and what the

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.520
implications would be, how likely it is to
happen.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:33.250
But before we even get into that as a general
principle, when we look at the stock market

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:40.340
based on presidential terms, my personal analysis
is that the stock market tends to return a

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.800
higher tends to see a higher return under
Democratic presidents than Republican presidents.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.760
It's not an overwhelming difference.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.100
So the first question is, is that distinction
relevant to presidential policy?

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.430
I do not think it is I think most of that
stuff, when you really look at the data, there's

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.930
a lot of noise in there.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.950
And there was like, obviously, you're right
that Democratic presidents tend to have a

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:08.610
slightly higher return, but it's also kind
of biased by certain things.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.500
You think about Hoover and it was like a Great
Depression.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.820
So that kind of messed things up.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.250
You think about it which time period you want
to do.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.670
Clinton probably has the best returns ever,
and that was the 90s.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.440
So like there's all these outliers that kind
of move things around a little bit.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.909
And of course, presidents do affect policy,
which affects markets.

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:25.290
I don't think it's as linked as we think.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.960
I mean, probably a lot more noise, unfortunately.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.710
Would it be fair to say then that the argument
you sometimes hear from the right that the

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:37.340
stock market is a disaster during Democratic
presidents?

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.060
That certainly isn't true.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.799
Yeah, I don't think that's true.

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:44.700
I think that the same is also true on the
other side on the disaster turned Democratic.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.010
I think that's all just political talk coming
out.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.549
One of the interesting things that you write
in your recent article about the Biden tax

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:58.560
plan is that there certainly is an impact
on one's personal finances and networth from

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.290
presidents, but that it often has much more
to do with tax policy than it does with the

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:03.689
ups and downs of the stock market.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:05.990
And that's a really important thing to understand.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:12.910
So when you start looking at what the economy
might be like from a personal finance standpoint,

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.020
are you focusing actually more on tax policy
than expected stock stock market movement?

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:22.140
Yeah, I mean, for like free for you and I
and for most people, like what how tax policy

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.231
changes is going to affect the dollars coming
into your accounts and how much dollars you

00:02:26.231 --> 00:02:27.231
have to pay out to the government.

00:02:27.231 --> 00:02:31.569
And that's going to have a much bigger impact
than, you know, how the market might necessarily

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:32.569
react.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:34.959
And of course, it's one of those things you
can't really know.

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:39.160
But I know tax policy for sure changes people's
decisions and behavior in a much bigger way

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.540
than I think just some minor moves in the
stock market.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.310
Well, so let's talk a little bit about the
Biden tax plan.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:51.410
When we did a dove into it a few weeks ago,
some of the notable things were the change

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:56.550
to the corporate income tax rate going from
twenty one to twenty eight percent, repealing

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.780
the Trump tax cuts.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:03.379
And you would see long term cap gains rates
go from about twenty four to close to 40 percent.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:06.160
You'd see that top marginal tax rate go up.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.080
I think it's about three points to the pre
Trump era.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:16.560
And then and then very importantly right now,
Social Security taxes stop at about one hundred

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.290
and thirty thousand dollars in income roughly.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:20.379
I don't I know that's not exactly right.

00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:25.890
The Biden proposal would then reinstate that
twelve point four percent tax from four hundred

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:32.409
thousand dollars and up completely uncapped,
which if done, is a massive amount of revenue

00:03:32.409 --> 00:03:35.810
for for the government to start with.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:40.239
Is it accurate that those under four hundred
thousand dollars a year in income will not

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:44.209
see an increase, which has been a big topic
of political debate?

00:03:44.209 --> 00:03:47.440
They should not see an increase in income
tax.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.260
Now, there are if everything in the plan went
through, there are certain ways in which,

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.360
for example, if you're getting property, if
you're inheriting property, you may have to

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:57.620
pay taxes on that.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:02.440
If there's unrealized gains that you wouldn't
have had to pay in the in the current state

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.440
of the world.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.450
So no income tax is going to be increased
on those people.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:11.310
But you might see other types of taxes show
up given other life events.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:16.230
As I said, if you're inheriting property specifically,
that's one that's going to be very relevant.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:21.449
And with that property inheritance scenario,
is that based on the size of the estate or

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:24.289
it could be regardless of the size of the
estate.

00:04:24.289 --> 00:04:26.479
So so there's a little bit of both.

00:04:26.479 --> 00:04:32.479
So the estate tax right now allows for eleven
point million, one point six million for a

00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:36.289
single person or twenty three point two for
a couple.

00:04:36.289 --> 00:04:39.349
You can pass on no estate tax.

00:04:39.349 --> 00:04:41.259
And then there's also something called stepped
up basis.

00:04:41.259 --> 00:04:46.130
What that means is, let's say, you know, your
grandparents had a stock and they bought Apple

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.879
in like, you know, nineteen ninety five or
whatever, and now it's got a million dollars

00:04:49.879 --> 00:04:50.879
in gains.

00:04:50.879 --> 00:04:53.119
They can when they die, they can pass that
on to their heirs.

00:04:53.119 --> 00:04:55.009
No taxes owed on the gain.

00:04:55.009 --> 00:04:58.639
But if they get rid of that rule, anyone,
regardless of who you are, you get that you're

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:00.229
going to have to pay the tax on the difference.

00:05:00.229 --> 00:05:02.259
And that's also true of real estate, among
other things.

00:05:02.259 --> 00:05:03.969
Now, I don't think that's actually going to
pass.

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:06.410
There's a lot of talk, a lot of tax professionals
about this like that.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:10.052
There's no way stepped up pace is going to
be completely gotten rid of because it's a

00:05:10.052 --> 00:05:11.052
very complex thing.

00:05:11.052 --> 00:05:14.849
I think the reason they use it to get rid
of stepped up basis is because it's just simpler

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:19.669
not to have to track how you improve the real
estate property for over 40 years and every

00:05:19.669 --> 00:05:23.210
single home repair and then try and track
the basis of that is a nightmare for from

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.009
the IRS and for individuals.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:26.759
So that's why they just kind of wipe it clean.

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.339
It's just a little bit easier that way.

00:05:28.339 --> 00:05:32.559
So I want to talk about the likelihood of
any of these different things passing now

00:05:32.559 --> 00:05:34.990
to even start that conversation.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:39.809
If Democrats don't take control of the Senate
with a 50 50 tie by getting the two Georgia

00:05:39.809 --> 00:05:45.059
runoffs, having Kamala Harris as the tie breaking
vote, does any of this stuff pass or is this

00:05:45.059 --> 00:05:46.059
all I mean?

00:05:46.059 --> 00:05:49.740
Is tax reform even getting done without a
Democratic Senate?

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:53.409
Yeah, so I don't think it's going to get to
I mean, even if they pass this.

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:58.249
No, here's that, here's the thing, even if
they do get you know, they won the two Georgia

00:05:58.249 --> 00:06:02.729
Senate races, Kamala Harris, you know, cast
the tie breaking vote, you still have to,

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:04.000
like, argue for all these things.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.129
This is like the wish list.

00:06:05.129 --> 00:06:06.599
All these different things are the wish list.

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:09.439
But what's going to happen is they're going
to go back and forth with Republicans and

00:06:09.439 --> 00:06:11.960
even there will be some Democrats who say,
I don't want to eliminate this or I don't

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:12.960
want to do this.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.610
So this is really like the best case scenario
just to start.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:16.770
And they're going to bargain in.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.289
Some of these things are going to come and
go.

00:06:18.289 --> 00:06:21.569
And I think, for example, stepped up basis
will probably be one of the things, if I had

00:06:21.569 --> 00:06:24.159
to guess, that gets kicked out first.

00:06:24.159 --> 00:06:28.360
So even so, for now, still continuing the
scenario where Democrats do have the Senate,

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:29.749
but there's negotiation.

00:06:29.749 --> 00:06:36.450
I have a really hard time imagining that Social
Security will be uncapped from 400000 and

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:37.450
up.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:42.929
And the reason I say that without making any
moral judgment about it is that is millions

00:06:42.929 --> 00:06:46.899
and tens of millions of dollars in taxes for
for a lot of people.

00:06:46.899 --> 00:06:50.610
And of course, part of it can be avoided by
not earning income through wages.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.089
And we know about the way that that that can
be done.

00:06:53.089 --> 00:06:55.300
I don't know how that gets passed.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.099
Even the Democratic Senate.

00:06:57.099 --> 00:06:58.490
Yeah, that's a good point.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:06:59.490
I think.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:00.490
Yeah, you're right.

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:04.979
In terms of kind of people will just immediately
companies like your new salary cap is four

00:07:04.979 --> 00:07:07.409
hundred thousand dollars and we're going to
pay you all this extra extra equity income

00:07:07.409 --> 00:07:08.719
for this extra thing.

00:07:08.719 --> 00:07:13.289
They're going to find ways to get money to
you in ways that are not classified as labor

00:07:13.289 --> 00:07:14.289
income.

00:07:14.289 --> 00:07:17.039
Now, I think some people are probably still
just going to take the hit and like, OK, you

00:07:17.039 --> 00:07:19.979
know, the employers take six percent, had
to pay six percent or whatever.

00:07:19.979 --> 00:07:22.680
But, yeah, a lot of people are going to shift
salaries around.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.699
And just like, oh, there's be the 400 K Club
out there to the Bronx that.

00:07:26.699 --> 00:07:29.099
So what's the most reliable way?

00:07:29.099 --> 00:07:32.809
I have sometimes financial advisors who will
write to me or they'll call into my show and

00:07:32.809 --> 00:07:36.530
they'll say, you know, we already know that
just increase increasing income tax rates

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:41.779
is not a great way to capture necessarily
more revenue because a lot of really wealthy

00:07:41.779 --> 00:07:44.430
people are not earning through wages.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.419
So then you've got to maybe look at capital
gains.

00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:50.340
But then there's also different ways to avoid
paying capital gains taxes.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.469
Or you can always just kind of wait it out
because with capital gains, a lot of it has

00:07:53.469 --> 00:07:55.419
to do with when are you realizing the gain?

00:07:55.419 --> 00:07:59.369
And if you don't like what you would pay,
now you can wait for a different administration

00:07:59.369 --> 00:08:03.439
in your experience, like if the government
was determined, we want to capture more of

00:08:03.439 --> 00:08:06.429
the different types of income that people
are making.

00:08:06.429 --> 00:08:10.279
What would be the most sort of ironclad way
to do it?

00:08:10.279 --> 00:08:11.280
I don't know, that's a great question.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:14.860
I think this is the question that's like everyone's
trying to figure out how do you curb inequality?

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.050
Really, that's really the question.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:17.050
How do you curb inequality?

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:19.000
And someone's like, OK, we can just raise
capital gains.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.800
Like, well, let's say let's say the you know,
the probability of this whole thing happening

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.849
where Democrats take over the Senate, let's
say it's like a ninety five percent chance

00:08:26.849 --> 00:08:29.289
right now, predict it's putting it like twenty
four point five percent.

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:31.160
There's a one in four chance right now.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:32.300
Let's say it was ninety five percent.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.909
What you're going to see is people start realizing
the gains.

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:36.150
Now they're going to start going through.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:40.890
There was even this thing where in 2010 think
the estate tax was like zero and a surprisingly

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:43.199
large number of people happened to die that
year.

00:08:43.199 --> 00:08:47.720
So it's like people literally changed their
lives and somehow just accept the grave to

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:48.720
avoid taxes.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.180
So I don't know what's the best way to do
that.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.750
I wish I had a better answer for you.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:54.500
Like you can do things like that.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.529
People have tried wealth taxes, but that's
like on the really, really rich people.

00:08:58.529 --> 00:09:03.269
But that's talk like how do you determine,
well, how do you capture that, prevent people

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:04.269
from fleeing?

00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:05.930
There's all sorts of ways to get at this.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:07.110
I think it's really difficult.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.839
I mean, capital gains are a start, but it
depends how big the jump is.

00:09:10.839 --> 00:09:14.370
I mean, a twenty three point eight to thirty
nine point six percent is a big jump, and

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:17.389
that's going to change people's behavior if
that were to happen.

00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:21.960
So then going to the next scenario, which
is Democrats don't get the Senate, is there

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:25.940
any tax reform getting done in the next two
years if Democrats don't get the Senate?

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:26.940
I don't think so.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:27.940
I mean, we're ending gridlock.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:32.500
I mean, I'm obviously not an expert at that
thing, but I don't see how it's going to happen

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:33.500
out.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:36.690
Republicans, if they can prevent it from happening,
they're happy with what's going on right now.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:39.379
So I think we'll just have more of the same.

00:09:39.379 --> 00:09:45.560
So for the average person who makes an average
salary, maybe has access to some kind of matching

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.579
contribution, if they're in education, maybe
it's a 403 B or a 401K.

00:09:50.579 --> 00:09:56.190
You know, with our staff, we do a simple IRA,
average person, a normal person.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:10:02.000
Does it make any sense to be making any changes
to what you are doing on the basis of who

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.350
is in the White House?

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:06.430
I would say no, definitely not.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:10.160
I mean, going back to our earlier discussion,
like, you know, markets rise, markets fall

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:11.850
under any sort of president.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.470
You can look through history like they have
drawdowns.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:14.470
Twenty, thirty percent drawdowns.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.980
Almost every single president and markets
go up during almost every president.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.160
I mean, there's obviously exceptions, you
know, but I don't think it's the president's

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:24.399
fault that, you know, we have hyperinflation
and we have this or that.

00:10:24.399 --> 00:10:27.139
Like, I don't think it's necessarily always
the president's fault for that type of thing.

00:10:27.139 --> 00:10:31.279
You know, even when FDR took over in the Great
Depression, like initially, like, there was

00:10:31.279 --> 00:10:32.279
a lot of hope.

00:10:32.279 --> 00:10:35.290
But still, the economy was sluggish for a
while and finally got back together.

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:42.360
So when the coronavirus when the initial coronavirus
dip happened in sort of like mid-March, the

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:50.360
Internet was riddled with hot takes about
cell now or by more options or weighed by

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.390
back in in a month, this sort of stuff.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:57.939
Now, my view, having read everything from
Ben Graham one hundred years ago and understanding

00:10:57.939 --> 00:11:03.460
what Warren Buffett's perspective is and the
great Jack Bogle and so on and so forth, my

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:07.140
view has always been, I don't think I can
time the market.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:08.380
I don't think I'm slick enough.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:15.430
I've got a full time job and understanding
that each year a huge portion of the gain

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:18.209
comes from a very small number of trading
days.

00:11:18.209 --> 00:11:22.459
And if you're timing, you could miss one of
those days and miss out on a lot of the return

00:11:22.459 --> 00:11:26.529
for a particular year, not to mention there
are tax implications to buying and selling,

00:11:26.529 --> 00:11:27.529
et cetera.

00:11:27.529 --> 00:11:32.910
Are there exceptions to that where it makes
sense to either try to time the market based

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:38.430
on the news cycle or legislatively or in any
other way?

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:41.670
I would generally say no, I would never try
to do something like that.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:45.230
There are certain quantitative models that
use sort of a trend following to look looking

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:46.810
like 200 day moving averages and that.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.680
But I don't want to get into all the technicalities
of that.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:52.689
There are some that do and even those, like
a lot of those models which traditionally

00:11:52.689 --> 00:11:56.839
work for the last twenty years, fail during
covid because it was such a quick decline

00:11:56.839 --> 00:12:02.520
and it came back so quickly that quick would
be recovery that the models had never seen

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.520
anything like that.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:05.990
And so they weren't they didn't have any data
to prepare for that to jump back in.

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:10.090
So you see a lot of people that actually the
best in the business, a trend following, got

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:13.470
a little bit burn this year, unfortunately,
but that's kind of that's what happens.

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:16.339
You know, it's just that's that's the nature
of markets.

00:12:16.339 --> 00:12:20.889
So briefly, just talking about corporate tax
now, as we've been talking all about kind

00:12:20.889 --> 00:12:25.650
of personal financial, there's this idea,
again, in the Biden tax plan of raising the

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:28.550
corporate income tax rate from twenty one
to twenty eight percent.

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:33.100
Now, a lot of small business owners, including
me, set up as an S corporation.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:34.870
All of our income is passed through.

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:40.209
In other words, the business itself is paying
some STATT to quote Donald Trump, some statutory

00:12:40.209 --> 00:12:43.689
amount, which I think in Massachusetts is
like four hundred and fifty dollars or something

00:12:43.689 --> 00:12:44.840
like that.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:49.839
And then basically I'm getting a K one as
an owner and I'm paying tax on my personal

00:12:49.839 --> 00:12:50.839
income taxes.

00:12:50.839 --> 00:12:54.290
So the corporate tax rate, I don't believe
actually affects me.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:56.180
So it's kind of a two part question.

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:58.839
Are small businesses affected by the corporate
tax rate?

00:12:58.839 --> 00:13:00.740
Who really is affected by that?

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.680
Yeah, so that's that.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:06.870
And I mean, really, you're right, there's
a lot a lot of income, especially among, you

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.540
know, higher income families is coming through,
partner.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:13.439
Ships and smaller businesses, not necessarily
corporate tax, is going to be for, I think,

00:13:13.439 --> 00:13:16.999
much larger businesses, so it will affect
certain people.

00:13:16.999 --> 00:13:20.009
But I think, as you said, a lot of people
have smaller businesses that you pass through

00:13:20.009 --> 00:13:23.430
as a lot of law firms are pastors or partnerships,
things like that.

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:27.819
So it's going to affect some people and it'll
probably affect the stock market in some way

00:13:27.819 --> 00:13:31.170
if that were to come to pass, because obviously
corporate tax means less profits.

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:32.410
So just simple as that.

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:36.379
So once again, this is how likely is this?

00:13:36.379 --> 00:13:37.379
As of right now?

00:13:37.379 --> 00:13:39.259
It doesn't seem too likely.

00:13:39.259 --> 00:13:42.459
One of the things that's never made sense
to me, and I've asked a lot of economists

00:13:42.459 --> 00:13:45.509
about this, you hear this talking point from
businesses.

00:13:45.509 --> 00:13:50.329
If you raise the corporate tax rate, it will
be bad for the economy and it will be bad

00:13:50.329 --> 00:13:54.149
in general because businesses won't invest
in growing their business.

00:13:54.149 --> 00:13:58.499
To me, it seems completely counterintuitive
because if I'm a business owner and I can

00:13:58.499 --> 00:14:04.769
take profits at a 20 percent tax rate or take
profits at a 30 percent tax rate, avoiding

00:14:04.769 --> 00:14:08.430
a 30 percent tax seems like something I would
be more inclined to do.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.749
And the way you avoid tax, of course, is by
making capital investment.

00:14:11.749 --> 00:14:17.939
So if you apply a demand side approach, wouldn't
a higher corporate tax rate encourage investment

00:14:17.939 --> 00:14:22.110
in a business because you're incentivized
to avoid what is a higher rate of tax?

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:25.610
Yeah, I mean, that's one way to look at it.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:30.709
You can also say, like, OK, well, if my taxes
someone who doesn't want to go, do you want

00:14:30.709 --> 00:14:31.709
you get it.

00:14:31.709 --> 00:14:32.709
You want 70 cents.

00:14:32.709 --> 00:14:33.709
You want 80 cents.

00:14:33.709 --> 00:14:35.149
Like, oh, I'll take 70 cents here and give
it to me.

00:14:35.149 --> 00:14:36.149
Right.

00:14:36.149 --> 00:14:37.480
So you're still making money either way?

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:42.449
I do think the logic for that argument is
correct, but it has to be at a much higher

00:14:42.449 --> 00:14:44.180
rate, I think going twenty one to twenty eight.

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:45.649
It's like, where are you on the curve?

00:14:45.649 --> 00:14:48.579
That's always the question is, is the big
debate like if we went from obviously twenty

00:14:48.579 --> 00:14:52.040
one to one hundred, you can see obviously
that that would completely devastate business

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.600
there.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:54.839
But where is that.

00:14:54.839 --> 00:14:56.939
How big is it measuring that?

00:14:56.939 --> 00:14:58.800
That's really the crux of the argument.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:14:59.980
And people argue on both sides.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:03.930
And I think if it was clear, we would just
no way that we wouldn't be debating it.

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:04.930
That's the problem.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:08.070
A lot of these debates, the reason our debates
is because it's not clear cut in the data

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:09.070
and it was clear cut.

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:10.070
We just wouldn't debate it anymore.

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:11.680
I think that's kind of how this works.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:12.680
Yeah.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:15.279
And there's a lot of I think there's a lot
of bad faith actors on both sides with these

00:15:15.279 --> 00:15:18.501
issues that want to create edge cases that
nobody's arguing for.

00:15:18.501 --> 00:15:24.000
Like, for example, people against the 15 dollar
minimum wage will often say, well, the fifteen's

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.240
better because people have more money.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:26.600
What about one hundred and fifty?

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:28.410
And of course, nobody's talking about 150.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.550
150 wouldn't be an equilibrium wage.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:34.220
150 would not be a wage that's in line with
cost of living.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:39.600
And I'm wondering if you also think that to
really discuss this stuff, we have to eliminate

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:43.800
these kind of strawman edge cases and think
about the meat of the curve as you're talking

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.209
about, not the extremes.

00:15:45.209 --> 00:15:49.350
Yeah, I don't think you can use exceptions
to me to try and make rules are already rules.

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:50.350
It's really tough.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:53.420
There are obviously, for example, one of the
biggest things in the investment literature

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:54.569
is this whole idea of the Black Swan.

00:15:54.569 --> 00:15:58.970
It's like, oh, you look at this average distribution
and the exception is the most important thing

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:00.519
because the exception changes everything.

00:16:00.519 --> 00:16:05.050
So I kind of agree with some of that logic
sometimes, but sometimes it's also a strawman.

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:07.809
When are you talking about or you talk about
in a risk context?

00:16:07.809 --> 00:16:10.529
We're talking about in a minimum wage context,
obviously yallock.

00:16:10.529 --> 00:16:11.529
Why not?

00:16:11.529 --> 00:16:12.529
One hundred fifty?

00:16:12.529 --> 00:16:13.550
Because that makes obviously no sense.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:18.569
Like there's too many jobs where the equilibrium
wage is just far too low compared to one hundred

00:16:18.569 --> 00:16:19.800
fifty an hour.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:21.510
So I completely see both sides of it.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:25.110
But yeah, I agree that you should try to like
where's the meat of the distribution.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:27.170
I think it's a good way of looking at it.

